Refusal in the Philippines: Situating Critical Theory at"}, {"id": "https://openalex.org/W2884912426", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1", "title": "Taking responsibility for health in an epistemically polluted environment", "publication_year": 2018, "type": "article", "cited_by_count": 48, "open_access": {"is_oa": true, "oa_status": "hybrid", "oa_url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1.pdf", "any_repository_has_fulltext": true}, "primary_location": {"id": "doi:10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1", "is_oa": true, "landing_page_url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1", "pdf_url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1.pdf", "source": {"id": "https://openalex.org/S4210179293", "display_name": "Metamedicine", "issn_l": "0166-2031", "issn": ["0166-2031", "1386-7415", "1573-0980", "1573-1200"], "is_oa": false, "is_in_doaj": false, "is_core": false, "listed_in": ["anvur-class-a", "anvur-scientific", "erih-plus", "jufo-1", "ki-jl-1", "medline", "norway-1", "poland-100", "russia-white-list-2", "vabb-shw"], "host_organization": "https://openalex.org/P4310319900", "host_organization_name": "Springer Science+Business Media", "host_organization_lineage": ["https://openalex.org/P4310319900", "https://openalex.org/P4310319965"], "host_organization_lineage_names": ["Springer Science+Business Media", "Springer Nature"], "type": "journal"}, "license": "cc-by", "license_id": "https://openalex.org/licenses/cc-by", "version": "publishedVersion", "is_accepted": true, "is_published": true, "raw_source_name": "Theoretical Medicine and Bioethics", "raw_type": "journal-article"}, "abstract": "Proposals for regulating or nudging healthy choices are controversial. Opponents often argue that individuals should take responsibility for their own health, rather than be paternalistically manipulated for their own good. In this paper, I argue that people can take responsibility for their own health only if they satisfy certain epistemic conditions, but we live in an epistemic environment in which these conditions are not satisfied. Satisfying the epistemic conditions for taking responsibility, I argue, requires regulation of this environment. I describe some proposals for such regulation and show that we cannot reject all regulation in the name of individual responsibility. We must either regulate individuals' healthy choices or regulate the epistemic environment."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "41ccd397b414923eb280087d33dcc7b31fd284e3e8a99b00b9f0991f62ea0c2a", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "judgment_and_consensus", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.1177/0309132516681485", "doi:10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1", "doi:10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1.pdf", "openalex:W2560541126", "openalex:W2884912426"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.1177/0309132516681485", "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1", "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11017-018-9444-1.pdf"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}
